1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,960
Tap send on twenty million dong to someone at your own bank, or across town at a rival bank, and both phones chime in three seconds.

2
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:10,010
Underneath, reserve money moves in completely opposite ways.

3
00:00:10,730 --> 00:00:14,410
In a same-bank On-Us transfer, zero reserve cash leaves the vault.

4
00:00:14,410 --> 00:00:22,050
The core server trims twenty million off your row, adds twenty million to theirs, and keeps 100% of those cheap customer deposits inside.

5
00:00:22,850 --> 00:00:26,710
Cross-bank Off-Us orders can't touch a rival bank's ledger.

6
00:00:26,710 --> 00:00:36,140
At ten PM on Saturday, the receiving bank still pays out in three seconds only because your bank locked pre-funded collateral inside the switch.

7
00:00:36,940 --> 00:00:42,610
At cutoff, the central bank doesn't wire twenty million every time someone buys lunch.

8
00:00:42,610 --> 00:00:49,530
The switch nets both sides: 500 billion sent out minus 470 billion sent back leaves just 30 billion dong.

9
00:00:50,130 --> 00:00:59,890
The central bank transfers only that 30 billion difference between master reserve accounts, sparing 940 billion dong in liquidity from moving back and forth all day.

10
00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:12,130
Massive 10-billion-dong wires skip retail netting and run one by one on RTGS, while stalled year-end transfers sit in Account 113 as cash in transit until January clears them.
